// ADR 0071 M1.4b slice 3b: a BOUND `let g = lock(m)` is now SOUND — the `?Guard`
// unlocks the mutex on its scope-exit drop, BEFORE the owning `Mutex`'s
// `sentinel_mutex_release`, so the cell is unlocked before it is freed.
//
// This is the slice-3b replacement for the prior slice-2b-ii fixture, which
// deliberately leaked (`is_some(lock(mutex_new(42)))`, an unbound rvalue) because
// binding + dropping a still-locked mutex tripped the runtime's free-while-locked
// debug-assert. The guard no-escape pin rule (a `lock()` may only be the direct RHS
// of an immutable `let`) also REJECTS that old unbound form, so the sound bound form
// is now the only spelling — see `tests/ui/c71_guard_not_let_bound.sentinel`.
//
// Trace: `mutex_new(42)` (rc=1, m owns); `lock(m)` acquires (uncontended → success)
// and yields a `?Guard` whose payload is the cell handle `m`; `is_some(g)` reads the
// valid bit (true) → 42. At main's scope exit the drops fire in reverse-declaration
// order: `g` first (`sentinel_mutex_unlock(m)` on the valid arm), then `m`
// (`sentinel_mutex_release`, rc→0, freed once). A clean exit 42 IS the leak/unlock
// check — a missed unlock would trip the runtime's free-while-locked debug-assert,
// and a miscounted refcount a use-after-free crash. (The `*g` deref that reads the
// protected value is slice 3c.) Exit 42.

fn main() -> i64 {
    let m = mutex_new(42);
    let g = lock(m);
    if is_some(g) {
        42
    } else {
        0
    }
}
